one if detection is unavailable. Independent of \code{nthreads} (BLAS) and \code{ncores}
(outcome fitting). See \code{\link{sis_glm}} for caps and serial fallback.
Ignored when screening is off.}

\item{validation}{Optional list for validation-based IRLS stopping and
batch/start selection, supported for all families and links. Supply
\code{X} and \code{y} for held-out observations, using the same predictor
columns and response format as the training data. Named predictors are
matched automatically. Optional \code{weights} and \code{offset} accept
scalars, observation vectors or matrices following the training formats.
Controls are \code{patience = 3L}, \code{minit = 3L}, \code{tol = 1e-8},
and a scoring-only \code{mu_floor = 1e-8} for positive-mean families.
Gaussian identity models compare \code{starts = c("default", "marginal")},
with conditional marginal slopes as a second initializer; other models
use \code{starts = "default"}. An explicit \code{start} restricts the
comparison to that start and preserves its exclusions. \code{rule = "min"}
chooses minimum validation error; \code{rule = "1se"} chooses the sparsest
candidate within one empirical standard error of the minimum.
The default \code{NULL} fits to the usual numerical stopping criterion.}

\item{precluster}{Enable response-channel preclustering, default \code{FALSE}.
Currently supports nonnegative identity-link Poisson models. See
\code{\link{preclustering}} for the fitting sequence and mathematical model.}

\item{cluster.refit}{With preclustering, refine selection on every original
channel (default \code{TRUE}). \code{FALSE} returns a model of summed cluster outcomes
at the requested lambda. This is separate from \code{relaxed}.}

\item{control.precluster}{Named advanced settings for clustering and
screening. Defaults include at most 32 groups, Ward Poisson clustering,
GIC cut selection, unit row bins and a screening factor of 0.1. See
\code{\link{preclustering}} for all entries.}
}
\value{
An object of class \code{L0glm_fit}, with list components below.
Special-family models additionally inherit from \code{L0glm_extended_fit}.
Use \code{coef()}, \code{logLik()}, \code{AIC()}, \code{BIC()},
\code{GIC()}, \code{EBIC()} and \code{MBIC()} to extract coefficients
and scores; see \link{model_criteria} and \link{L0glm.metrics}.
\code{print()} shows a compact overview; \code{summary()} adds fit metrics
and a bounded coefficient display; see \link{model_summary}.
\code{predict()} and \code{fitted()} provide original-scale predictions;
see \link{model_predictions} for new data, offsets and special-family types.
\item{\code{coefficients}}{a sparse dgCmatrix with the fitted coefficients
(single column for the univariate case). If `intercept = TRUE`, the first 
row is for the intercept.}
\item{\code{weights}}{final IRLS working weights for ordinary GLMs and
native Poisson; prior observation weights for special-family models.}
\item{\code{prior.weights}, \code{offset}}{original prior observation
weights and offsets retained for ordinary-model scoring. Special-family
models retain prior weights in \code{weights}.}
\item{\code{dispersion}, \code{dispersion.estimated}}{fitted dispersions
and flags distinguishing estimated from fixed dispersion for ordinary GLMs.
Special families retain their nuisance-parameter information separately.}
\item{\code{init.lambda}}{initial lambda.} \item{\code{lambdas}}{a vector or
matrix with the adaptive penalties lambdas.} \item{\code{family}}{The error
structure used to fit the data.}
\item{\code{z}}{a vector or matrix containing the working response in the
final iteration of \code{L0glm.fit}}
\item{\code{X}}{design matrix X, including the intercept column if added}
\item{\code{y}}{response vector or matrix y.}
\item{\code{p}}{original number of selectable predictors.}
\item{\code{screening}}{screening method, batch size, candidate/retained rows,
outer iteration history, best round and stopping information when screening
is used; otherwise absent (\code{NULL}).}
\item{\code{validation}}{when requested, validation deviance and separate
\code{selection} and \code{relaxed} diagnostics, including the best iteration,
number of attempted iterations, stopping flag and per-iteration history.
Multichannel ordinary models also retain pass/channel diagnostics.
The \code{starts} table records each start/batch candidate's validation
deviance, fitting time and nonzero count; \code{selected_start} and
\code{selected_nsis} identify the returned model. \code{minimum},
\code{se}, \code{rule} and \code{scoring} describe the selection criterion.}
\item{\code{always.include}}{indices of unpenalized mandatory coefficients,
including recognized intercepts, in the returned coefficient matrix.}
\item{\code{preprocessing}}{training column centers and scales, resolved
normalization mode, centering eligibility, and original selectable count.
Coefficients are already on the original input scale.}
\item{\code{convergence}}{one row per channel and selection or relaxed pass,
containing its stage, pass, channel, iteration count, convergence flag,
number of dispersion updates and number of reactivations after a freeze.
The flag records whether the iterative stopping criterion was met.}
}
\description{
Fits a generalized linear model with an \eqn{L_0} penalty or,
for multichannel responses, a grouped row-\eqn{L_0} penalty, equivalent
to an \eqn{L_{2,0}} penalty, to approximate Best Subset Selection (for
univariate data) or Best Subset of Groups Selection (for multivariate data)
subject to optional nonnegativity constraints on model coefficients.
The objective functions that can be approximated are :
\itemize{

\item for univariate data, using an \eqn{L_0} norm penalty to achieve Best Subset Selection:

\deqn{-2\,LL(\beta) + \lambda \|\beta\|_0}

where \eqn{LL} is the log-likelihood of the GLM model and
\eqn{\|\beta\|_0} is the number of non-zero elements in
coefficient vector beta, i.e. the \eqn{L_0} norm of beta.

\item for multivariate data, using independent elementwise \eqn{L_0}
penalized fits on each outcome:

\deqn{-2\,LL(B) + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p}\sum_{k=1}^{m} I(B_{jk} \neq 0)}

\item for multivariate data, using grouped variable selection in multitask
learning settings to achieve Best Subset of Groups Selection:

\deqn{-2\,LL(B) + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p} I(\|B_{j\cdot}\|_2 > 0)}

}

Here \eqn{B} is the \eqn{p \times m} multichannel coefficient matrix and the
grouped penalty counts active rows, i.e. predictors with a non-zero
coefficient in at least one channel. Any positive within-row norm gives the
same row support, so this is equivalently group-\eqn{L_0}, row-\eqn{L_0}, or
\eqn{L_{2,0}} regularization.

In all three cases, \code{nonnegative = TRUE} restricts the coefficients to
be nonnegative, including any intercept, except that binomial and
quasibinomial intercepts remain unrestricted:

\deqn{\beta_j \geq 0 \quad\hbox{or}\quad B_{jk} \geq 0.}
With \code{nonnegative = FALSE}, coefficients may have either sign.

These objectives are approximated via an iterative adaptive ridge regression
procedure, see Details. The univariate and independent multichannel
elementwise \eqn{L_0} penalties can be used to approximate Best Subset
Selection, while for multivariate (multitask/multichannel) data, the
group-\eqn{L_0}/row-\eqn{L_0}/\eqn{L_{2,0}} penalty can be used to approximate
Best Subset of Groups Selection (via option \code{grouped=TRUE}). Fits on
multichannel data can be parallelized. Option \code{relaxed} allows one to
finish with a (nearly) unpenalized fit on the \code{L0glm} selected features.

The level of regularisation can be manually chosen (if a numeric value is
passed to \code{lambda}) or preset to approximate an information criterion.
Criterion names are case-insensitive. See \link{information_criteria} for
the penalty formulas, guidance on choosing a criterion and original references.

The \code{L0glm.fit} function has various advanced features, including
support for both dense and sparse design matrices \emph{\bold{X}}, high
dimensional data (where \emph{p} >> \emph{n}), single or multichannel
outcomes \emph{\bold{y}} (with either independent or grouped variable
selection), support for any of the standard R GLM families & link functions,
prior weights (e.g. total nr. of trials for binomial proportions), model
offsets, starting coefficient values or dispersion (if prior estimate is
available, otherwise optimised), optional nonnegativity constraints,
unpenalized terms (e.g. intercept) and shrinkage towards given prior means
(via targeted nonzero-centered ridge regression updates).

A choice of several fast C++ coded least squares solvers can be used, which
means this function scales well to solve fairly large tall or wide problems
with millions of observations or variables. As solvers, we currently
implemented the \code{Eigen} sparse or dense Cholesky, QR, PCG & LSCG plus 
BDCSVD and Jacobi SVD solvers, as well as two solvers that natively support 
nonnegativity constraints: \code{bigGlm} from \pkg{glmnet}, and \code{osqp}, a fast
quadratic programming solver.
}
\details{
L0glm approximates the \eqn{L_0}, independent multichannel
elementwise \eqn{L_0}, or grouped row-\eqn{L_0}/\eqn{L_{2,0}} penalized GLM
objectives via an iterative adaptive ridge regression procedure.

\strong{Algorithm for univariate, single-channel data}

For univariate data, the approach builds on L0 penalized
regression by de Rooi & Eilers (2011), Frommlet & Nuel (2016), Liu et al.
(2017), Dai et al. (2018 & 2020) & Li et al. (2021) and is based in a simple
modification of the IRLS (Iteratively Reweighted Least Squares) algorithm used
to fit GLMs (cf. McCullagh & Nelder 1989, Hastie & Pregibon 1992, Hardin &
Hilbe 2007). In this algorithm, each iteration of the IRLS algorithm updates
the coefficient estimates using a quadratic approximation of the log
likelihood of the GLM objective. This is done using a weighted least squares
step. In our adapted algorithm, this step is replaced by a (zero or nonzero
centered) adaptive ridge regularised regression with optional
nonnegativity constraints on the coefficients. In each iteration, this uses
the following ridge penalties for the penalized terms (excluding the
intercept, if included):
\deqn{\Lambda = (1/4)\lambda\sigma^2 w}
with adaptive penalty weights
\deqn{w = 1 / |\beta|^2}
where \eqn{\beta} are the coefficient estimates
in each iteration. During selection, the relative cutoff and absolute
numerical floor specified by \code{thresh} remove coefficients from later solves.
The final relaxed refit does not apply this hard cutoff. The inverse-square
weights follow Frommlet & Nuel (2016). Nonnegativity
constraints are natively supported if the \code{bigGlm} or
the \code{osqp} solver is used, or are obtained via clipping if the
\code{eigen} solvers are used. The factor of 1/4 was shown to be required to
approximate the L0 penalty objective and has been proven for the Gaussian
case and under orthogonality (Frommlet & Nuel 2016), but in practice also
works well for non-orthogonal designs and other noise families.
In an IRLS step, fixed working weights and dispersion give a weighted
least-squares quadratic with curvature \eqn{h_j=x_j^T W x_j/\phi}.
For weighted-orthogonal columns its L0 selection boundary is
\eqn{h_j\widehat b_j^2=\lambda}; the adaptive-ridge fixed-point equation
\eqn{b_j^2-\widehat b_j b_j+\lambda/(4h_j)=0} has the same boundary.
Thus the quarter factor carries over directly to each working quadratic.
Standard likelihood regularity conditions motivate this approximation
increasingly well as sample size grows on an identifiable active model.
Correlated predictors couple the joint support decisions. If desired,
\code{L0glm.tune()} searches around the preset using the information criterion
or cross-validated deviance. The Algorithm and theory vignette details the
quadratic and large-sample arguments.
Dispersion (the variance for Gaussian models) can be fixed or estimated
during IRLS using \code{dispersion = NULL} or a negative initial value.

\subsection{Adaptive ridge weights}{
The exponent is fixed at two. Weights use normalized working coefficients
\eqn{b} relative to zero. Ordinary single-channel ridge diagonals are
\eqn{D_j=(\lambda/4)\phi/|b_j|^2}, with scale dispersion \eqn{\phi}.
Special families have scale dispersion one. Grouped multichannel NB and
multinomial fits use \eqn{D_{jc}=\lambda/(4\|b_j\|_2^2)}, shared across
each row. For multinomial models the norm includes all \code{K+1} CLR
category coefficients. Ungrouped NB and multinomial fits use
\eqn{D_{jc}=\lambda/(4|b_{jc}|^2)}; Cox and single-channel NB use the
same scalar rule. Mandatory terms have zero ridge penalty and excluded
coefficients remain excluded. Hard thresholding is controlled separately
by \code{thresh}. Special-family relaxed refits use \code{lambda = 0}.
}

\subsection{Optimization interpretation}{
Adaptive ridge uses successive quadratic penalties to promote sparsity.
Abergel, Bouaziz & Nuel (2024) derive a variational representation for
\eqn{L_q} penalties and explain their majorization--minimization interpretation.
With zero ridge centers and fixed dispersion, inverse-square
weights give tangent quadratic penalties for a log penalty on the current
nonzero coefficients. The inequality
\deqn{\log s \leq \log s_t + (s-s_t)/s_t,\qquad s,s_t>0,}
with \eqn{s=b_j^2}, yields a quadratic coefficient proportional to
\eqn{1/(b_j^{(t)})^2}. For grouped norms the same argument uses
\eqn{s=\|b_j\|_2^2}. These inverse-square weights give the broken
adaptive ridge rule.
IRLS approximates the likelihood, and hard thresholding reduces subsequent
systems to the active support. Information criteria evaluate the resulting
models using their likelihood and support size. Exact surrogate minimization
with a fixed likelihood and penalty gives the usual MM descent argument;
the package also accommodates dispersion updates, constraints and relaxed
refits as described below.
}

\subsection{Hierarchical Bayesian and ARD interpretation}{
A ridge update is a conditional maximum a posteriori (MAP) estimate under
Gaussian coefficient priors. With the default zero ridge center, the
inverse-square update corresponds to prior precision
\eqn{\alpha_j=\lambda/(4\beta_j^2)} in the solver's coefficient coordinates;
its ridge coefficient is \eqn{\phi\alpha_j} for GLM dispersion \eqn{\phi}.
The prior remains zero-centered and skeptical: small fitted effects receive
concentrated priors and strong effects receive wider priors. The next IRLS
step updates the coefficient mode using a local quadratic likelihood.
This gives a plug-in empirical-Bayes-style connection to automatic relevance
determination (ARD), with shared precisions providing a group-ARD view.
Evidence-maximizing ARD also accounts for posterior uncertainty; the adaptive
ridge rule here updates scales from coefficient point estimates. The ridge
center stays fixed; \code{prior.mean} specifies its value.
See Tipping (2001) and the Algorithm and theory vignette for this connection.
}

Automatic initialization uses one weak ridge fit to the initial working
response, with columns scaled to unit weighted norm and ridge constant
\code{1e-6}. Four reproducible random-sign probes share this factorization
to estimate the residual noise degrees of freedom,
\eqn{\operatorname{tr}\{(I-H)^T(I-H)\}}, where \eqn{H} is the pilot hat matrix.
The caller's random-number state is preserved. The pilot residual scale is
divided by \code{max(1, preset.lambda("gic", p, n))} for a permissive start
independent of the fitted lambda; its support is never
imposed on the main fit. A supplied negative value bypasses this pilot.
With preprocessing enabled, mandatory terms are profiled out and column
information is conditional on them, preserving changes of predictor origin
when an unrestricted intercept is present.
The normalized pilot also checks the effective number of residual
directions, \eqn{\nu=\operatorname{tr}(R^2)^2/\operatorname{tr}(R^4)},
with \eqn{R=I-H}. If \eqn{\nu<\min(10,(N-q)/5)}, where \eqn{q} is the
rank of the mandatory terms, or the estimated variance is below
\code{1e-6} times the nuisance-adjusted working-response variance, one
retry uses pilot ridge \code{0.1}. This stabilizes initialization near
interpolation. The probes reuse the same factorization, and ordinary
pilots pass a trace bound without additional solves.

Starting at iteration 3, when the number \eqn{k} of active coefficients is
below \eqn{0.7N}, dispersion is updated toward
\deqn{\widetilde\phi =
  \frac{\sum_{i:w_i>0}w_i(y_i-\mu_i)^2/V(\mu_i)}{N-k}.}
Here \eqn{N} counts positive-weight observations, \eqn{k} includes the
intercept, and \eqn{V} is \code{family$variance}, evaluated at the newly
fitted mean. Each update is bounded to \eqn{[\phi/2,2\phi]}. After three
consecutive updates with unchanged support and a target within 1\% of the
current dispersion, it is held fixed until the support changes again.
A later support loss reactivates these bounded updates in the same IRLS loop.
Each subsequent grouped selection pass may update it again on the new support.
A relaxed refit holds dispersion fixed during its coefficient solves and
reports the final Pearson residual scale for estimated channels; user-supplied
positive dispersions remain fixed throughout.

These inexpensive Pearson moment updates use the active coefficient count
to approximate residual degrees of freedom. In nearly interpolating models,
little residual information is available to estimate noise; if the active
model remains too large for an update, the starting scale is retained.
The pilot normally requires one factorization (two with the rare retry);
subsequent scale updates require only
\eqn{O(N)} arithmetic, without nested fitting or further factorizations.
Supplying a dispersion value avoids the pilot factorization, which can be
appreciable for very large dense designs even when the main solver is iterative.

\subsection{Shared numerical safeguards}{
Ordinary GLMs start their working model with \code{family$initialize}. Each ridge solve
proposes a coefficient update, followed by a shared backtracking check.
Dispersion and adaptive ridge penalties \eqn{d_j} are held fixed during
this check. For ordinary GLMs its target is
\deqn{Q(\beta)=D(y,\mu(\beta))+
  \sum_{j:d_j<\infty}d_j(\beta_j-a_j)^2,}
where \eqn{a_j} is \code{prior.mean}. Special families use their joint
log likelihood in the corresponding fixed-penalty target.
Smooth updates on an unchanged support must decrease this target within
numerical tolerance. If necessary, the coefficient step is halved until
its means are valid and its objective is finite and acceptable. Predictor
differences are reused, without another ridge solve or factorization.
A feasible backtracking anchor leaves the requested first-step penalty
weights and coefficient eligibility intact.

Hard thresholding follows the smooth update as a discrete selection step.
Bound clipping can also remove coefficients; these support changes require
finite valid predictions but can increase the smooth ridge target. This
preserves the adaptive-ridge selection path while safeguarding continuous
refits. A threshold deletion is retained only when it leaves valid means.
Starting-zero exclusions and coefficient bounds remain in force; relaxed
refits apply no hard threshold. Failed backtracking retains the previous
finite coefficients with a warning and \code{converged=FALSE}.

Distribution-specific kernels provide valid parameter domains, working
weights and stable deviance evaluations. For example, the log-link inverse
Gaussian uses \eqn{D=\sum_i w_i(y_i/\mu_i-1)^2/y_i} and
\eqn{W_i=w_i/\mu_i} to avoid intermediate overflow. The step-acceptance
rule is shared across families and the compiled Poisson implementation.
See Marschner (2011) for deviance backtracking in ordinary GLMs.
}

Fisher scoring uses the expected Hessian in each quadratic approximation.
If option \code{relaxed} is set to \code{TRUE} the algorithm
will refit the \code{L0glm} selected variables using a nearly unpenalized
model (with \code{lambda=1E-30}) without hard thresholding. This can reduce bias and
increase sensitivity. If an appropriate level of regularisation lambda is chosen,
the L0 norm penalized solution approximates Best Subset selection. To achieve
variable selection consistency, the \code{"bic"} \code{lambda} preset is
recommended for low dimensional settings, whilst \code{"gic"},
\code{"ebic"} or \code{"mbic"} may work better for high
dimensional settings, subject to the conditions discussed in
\link{information_criteria}. Setting it to
\code{"aic"} is recommended if predictive performance is of key interest, as
the AIC criterion asymptotically approximates the leave-one-out
cross-validation error.

\strong{Algorithm for multivariate/multichannel data}

For the multivariate ("multitask learning") case there are two options. First,
if \code{grouped=FALSE}, an independent \code{L0glm} model is fit on each of
the responses, which corresponds to an elementwise \eqn{L_0} penalty on the
coefficient matrix.
Second, if \code{grouped=TRUE}, grouped variable selection will be performed
via a row-\eqn{L_0} penalty, conventionally denoted \eqn{L_{2,0}} in the
multitask/MMV literature. This is achieved by taking the independent
elementwise \eqn{L_0} solution, and then, for all variables that have nonzero
coefficients in at least one of the channels, repeating the \eqn{L_0}
penalized fits but using a shared row-level start derived from the
coefficients across all channels (response variables) so as to achieve a
group penalty and perform grouped feature selection. Active rows are detected
by a row-wise nonzero count, equivalently by any positive within-row norm.
With signed coefficients, the shared start uses mean absolute magnitude
with the sign of the row mean. If that mean is exactly zero, the sign of the
first largest-magnitude entry is used. Thus opposite signs across channels
cannot reduce the starting magnitude by cancellation. Nonnegative fits retain
arithmetic averaging.
This is then further iterated until convergence. Finally, as for single
channel data, if \code{relaxed=TRUE}, the algorithm can finish with a final
step whereby the selected variables are refit using a nearly unpenalized model
(with \code{lambda=1E-10}). Compared to other group penalized regression
methods, such as the group LASSO or multitask lasso, which use an
\eqn{L_{2,1}} penalty, this approach produces a sparser solution that better
approximates the Best Subset of Groups solution if lambda is adequately
chosen.

\subsection{Screening}{
Screening reuses \code{\link{sis_glm}} for all ordinary and special families.
With screening enabled, omitted \code{lambda}, \code{relaxed} and
\code{parallelize} use \code{"gic"}, \code{TRUE} and \code{FALSE},
respectively. Explicit arguments are preserved. Other defaults are unchanged.
This reproduces the small-batch ISIS workflow used in the ultra-wide
Gaussian benchmark with a single \code{L0glm.fit(X, y)} call.

Each candidate model starts afresh. The original offsets and prior observation
weights are used for refitting; the fitted linear predictor is added to the
offset only for the next screen. The best GIC round is returned, or the last
round for quasi families without an available GIC. Penalty presets and GIC
always use the original selectable predictor count, before screening.
Explicit starting-zero exclusions remain in force, including per-channel
exclusions, while mandatory terms stay eligible. Coefficients and predictions
retain the original dimensions and units; screened-out coefficients are zero.
Sparse designs stay sparse and dense designs stay dense.

\code{screening} records the resolved method, automatic-dispatch flag,
batch size, candidates, retained indices, outer history and chosen round.
It also records the number of OpenMP screening threads actually used.
Outer stopping describes the screening search; inner convergence describes
each fitted model. Use \code{screen="none"} to fit the full design directly.
}

\subsection{Special families}{
The constructors \code{mgcv::nb()}, \code{mgcv::multinom(K)} and
\code{mgcv::cox.ph()} select family-specific IRLS implemented using this
package's ridge kernels. NB estimates one shape per channel unless fixed in
the constructor. Multinomial fits return all K+1 category coefficients in CLR
coordinates, with a default free intercept and invariant grouped row penalties.
Cox fits use right-censored responses, Breslow ties, no intercept, cached risk
sets and automatic LLT/matrix-free PCG routing. See
\link{L0glm_negative_binomial}, \link{L0glm_multinomial} and \link{L0glm_cox}
for the mathematics, examples and controls. These family loops
are serial: \code{parallelize}, \code{ncores}, \code{openmp} and
\code{group.maxit} do not govern them. Ordinary GLM and native Poisson paths
use the \code{parallelize}, \code{ncores} and \code{openmp} controls.
}

\subsection{Validation-based stopping}{
\code{validation=list(X=V,y=v)} tracks held-out deviance using a common
stopping rule. Ordinary GLMs use \code{family$dev.resids}, averaged per
weighted response; Gaussian identity therefore uses mean squared error.
Multichannel models aggregate response-channel deviances. Multinomial models
use joint deviance per weighted trial, and Cox models use partial deviance
per weighted event with risk sets formed from the validation observations.
For negative binomial models with estimated shape, the saturated reference
is fixed at the Poisson saturated likelihood, so changing training theta
cannot shift the reference between iterations. This gives a predictive
log-likelihood score; a fixed theta uses ordinary NB deviance.
After at least \code{minit} iterations, \code{patience} consecutive iterations
without an improvement exceeding \code{tol * max(1, best_deviance)} stop the
pass. The best checkpoint is returned, including its dispersion and penalties.
A training-only nuisance fit supplies the initial checkpoint. Relaxed refitting starts from
the selected checkpoint and can retain it if refitting increases validation
error. SIS/ISIS score their candidate rounds on the same validation set.
A vector \code{nsis}, with SIS/ISIS enabled, compares its batch sizes at
the requested lambda. Each candidate starts afresh, and the nuisance-only
model is included in the comparison. Automatic starts compare the default
initializer and, for Gaussian identity models, uncontracted conditional
marginal slopes. Starts use training data only. An explicit start restricts
the comparison to that start.
For \code{rule="1se"}, the standard error uses per-observation squared
validation losses from the minimum-error candidate; Cox uses event
contributions. Among eligible models,
fewer nonzero coefficients take priority, followed by a smaller batch size.
With \code{nsis=NULL}, each screened candidate uses the usual default batch.
Coefficient updates use training data only. Validation selects the stopping
point, so use separate test observations to assess prediction performance.
}
}
\examples{
# Single-channel Poisson deconvolution: shifted copies of a known PSF.
single <- simul(method=2, n=500, p=500, s=.1, seed=2)
fit_single <- L0glm.fit(single$X, single$y, family=poisson(identity),
  lambda="gic", nonnegative=TRUE, relaxed=TRUE, parallelize=FALSE)
L0glm.metrics(fit_single, beta_true=single$beta_true)[
  c("gic", "TP", "FP", "FN", "TN", "F1_score", "MCC")]

old_par <- par(no.readonly=TRUE)
par(mfrow=c(1, 1), mgp=c(2, .7, 0))
# The default PSF has peak one. Scale both spike trains by the same PSF peak
# to display them in signal units; negative values indicate mirroring only.
spike_scale <- max(single$X)
beta_single <- as.numeric(coef(fit_single))
mu_single <- as.numeric(fitted(fit_single))
height <- max(as.numeric(single$y), as.numeric(single$y_true), mu_single,
  as.numeric(single$beta_true)*spike_scale, beta_single*spike_scale)
plot(single$x, single$y, type="l", col="grey65", ylim=c(-1, 1)*height*1.15,
  xlab="Time", ylab="Signal / spike height (fit mirrored)",
  main="Single-channel Poisson deconvolution")
abline(h=0, col="grey75", lty=3)
lines(single$x, single$y_true, col="grey30", lty=2)
segments(single$x_beta, 0, single$x_beta,
  as.numeric(single$beta_true)*spike_scale, col="navy")
lines(single$x, -mu_single, col="orange", lwd=1.5)
segments(single$x_beta, 0, single$x_beta, -beta_single*spike_scale, col="red")
legend("topright", c("Observed signal", "True signal", "True spikes",
  "Fitted signal (mirrored)", "Fitted spikes (mirrored)"),
  col=c("grey65", "grey30", "navy", "orange", "red"),
  lty=c(1, 2, 1, 1, 1), bty="n", cex=.75)

# Multichannel Poisson deconvolution: select rows shared across channels.
# Retain every mass channel generated by the simulation.
multi <- simul(method=4, n=500, p=500, s=.05, seed=2)
fit_multi <- L0glm.fit(multi$X, multi$y, family=poisson(identity),
  lambda="gic", nonnegative=TRUE, grouped=TRUE, relaxed=TRUE,
  parallelize=FALSE)
L0glm.metrics(fit_multi, beta_true=multi$beta_true)[
  c("gic", "TP", "FP", "FN", "TN", "F1_score", "MCC",
    "within_row_F1_score", "within_row_MCC")]
# The within-row scores assess channel sparsity among correctly recovered rows.
dim(coef(fit_multi))                  # original predictors by response channels
par(mfrow=c(2, 1))
plotmat(multi$beta_true, xlab="Spike location", ylab="Channel",
  main="True multichannel coefficients")
plotmat(coef(fit_multi), xlab="Spike location", ylab="Channel",
  main="Estimated multichannel coefficients")
par(old_par)

# Precluster into at most 32 channel groups, select on their summed counts,
# then refine and return coefficients for every original mass channel.
system.time(fit_multi_clustered <- L0glm.fit(multi$X, multi$y,
  family=poisson(identity), lambda="gic", nonnegative=TRUE,
  grouped=TRUE, relaxed=TRUE, parallelize=TRUE, ncores=4L,
  precluster=TRUE, cluster.refit=TRUE,
  control.precluster=list(k.max=32L, rowbins=5L)))
fit_multi_clustered$preclustering$clusters
fit_multi_clustered$preclustering$seconds
L0glm.metrics(fit_multi_clustered, beta_true=multi$beta_true)[
  c("gic", "TP", "FP", "FN", "TN", "F1_score", "MCC",
    "within_row_F1_score", "within_row_MCC")]
dim(coef(fit_multi_clustered))         # all original predictors and channels

# A smooth nonnegative background: append a low-df spline basis and keep
# those columns unpenalized. Poisson backgrounds add directly to the signal.
B <- splines::bs(seq(0, 1, length.out=nrow(multi$X)), df=6, intercept=TRUE)
alpha <- outer(c(2, 5, 3, 7, 4, 2), seq(.5, 1.5, length.out=ncol(multi$y)))
set.seed(3)
Y_background <- multi$y + matrix(rpois(length(multi$y), B \%*\% alpha),
  nrow(multi$y), ncol(multi$y))
X_background <- cbind(multi$X, Matrix::Matrix(B, sparse=TRUE))
compulsory <- ncol(multi$X) + seq_len(ncol(B))
fit_background <- L0glm.fit(X_background, Y_background,
  family=poisson(identity), lambda="gic", nonnegative=TRUE,
  always.include=compulsory, grouped=TRUE, relaxed=TRUE,
  precluster=TRUE, ncores=4L,
  control.precluster=list(k.max=32L, rowbins=5L))
background_hat <- B \%*\% as.matrix(coef(fit_background)[compulsory, , drop=FALSE])
# Every original mass channel is retained in both the peak and background fit.
dim(coef(fit_background))
fit_background$nuisance$seconds
L0glm.metrics(fit_background,
  beta_true=rbind(multi$beta_true, Matrix::Matrix(alpha, sparse=TRUE)))[
  c("gic", "TP", "FP", "FN", "TN", "F1_score", "MCC",
    "within_row_F1_score", "within_row_MCC")]

# High-dimensional Gaussian regression with positive and negative effects.
# Run manually: the dense design alone occupies about 4 GB.
\dontrun{
n <- 500L
p <- 1000000L
set.seed(123)
X_hd <- matrix(rnorm(n*p), n, p)
support <- sample.int(p, 60L)
beta_hd <- numeric(p)
beta_hd[support] <- runif(60L, -.8, .8)
mean_hd <- as.numeric(X_hd[, support, drop=FALSE] \%*\% beta_hd[support])
set.seed(1)
y_hd <- mean_hd + rnorm(n)

# screen="auto" activates ISIS exactly when p_eligible > 10*n. Screened fits
# default to the original-p GIC preset and a relaxed refit.
# Screening threads are independent of BLAS threads (nthreads).
system.time(fit_hd <- L0glm.fit(X_hd, y_hd, screen.nthreads=8, nthreads=1))
fit_hd$screening[c("method", "screen_size", "iterations", "best_iteration", "nthreads")]
fit_hd$screening$history
L0glm.metrics(fit_hd, beta_true=beta_hd)[
  c("gic", "TP", "FP", "FN", "TN", "F1_score", "MCC")]
dim(coef(fit_hd))                    # all p rows, including screened-out zeros
# Explicit alternatives: screen="SIS", "ISIS", or "none".
}

# Basic controls: intercept constraints, dispersion and binomial fits.
x <- seq(-1, 1, length.out=30)
X <- matrix(x, ncol=1)

# Nonnegativity includes the Gaussian intercept.
constrained <- L0glm.fit(X, -2+3*x, family=gaussian(),
  intercept=TRUE, nonnegative=TRUE, lambda=1e-8,
  dispersion=1, parallelize=FALSE)
as.numeric(constrained$coefficients)

# Estimate Gaussian variance from an automatic or user-supplied start.
set.seed(1)
y_gaussian <- 2*x + rnorm(length(x), sd=.5)
automatic <- L0glm.fit(X, y_gaussian, family=gaussian(),
  lambda="gic", dispersion=NULL, maxit=100, parallelize=FALSE)
initialized <- L0glm.fit(X, y_gaussian, family=gaussian(),
  lambda="gic", dispersion=-.25, maxit=100, parallelize=FALSE)
# dispersion=.25 instead keeps the variance fixed at .25.

# A binomial intercept may remain negative with a nonnegative slope.
y <- round(1000*plogis(-2+x))/1000
logistic <- L0glm.fit(X, y, family=binomial(),
  weights=rep(1000, length(y)), nonnegative=TRUE,
  lambda=1e-8, parallelize=FALSE)
as.numeric(logistic$coefficients)

# Gaussian validation-based stopping: keep a separate test set for assessment.
set.seed(42)
X_validation <- matrix(rnorm(200*40), 200, 40)
y_validation <- 3 + 2*X_validation[, 1] - X_validation[, 2] + rnorm(200)
train <- 1:120
fit_stopped <- L0glm.fit(X_validation[train, ], y_validation[train],
  intercept=TRUE, lambda="gic", relaxed=TRUE, maxit=100,
  validation=list(X=X_validation[-train, ], y=y_validation[-train]),
  parallelize=FALSE)
fit_stopped$validation$selection[c("best_iteration", "iterations", "stopped")]

# Optionally select the ISIS batch on the same validation set.
# Omit nsis to keep the GIC-based default batch.
fit_batch <- L0glm.fit(X_validation[train, ], y_validation[train],
  intercept=TRUE, lambda="gic", screen="ISIS", nsis=c(1, 3, 5, 10),
  relaxed=TRUE, maxit=100, validation=list(
    X=X_validation[-train, ], y=y_validation[-train], rule="1se"),
  parallelize=FALSE)
fit_batch$validation[c("selected_start", "selected_nsis", "deviance")]
fit_batch$validation$starts
# Sparse special families: GIC preset fits, GIC tuning and three-fold CV.
if (requireNamespace("mgcv", quietly=TRUE)) {
  set.seed(204)
  n <- 300L
  X_special <- matrix(rnorm(n*20), n, 20)
  folds_special <- sample(rep(1:3, length.out=n))
  family_par <- par(mgp=c(2, .7, 0))

  # Negative binomial: two nonzero slopes among 20 predictors.
  mu_nb <- exp(.4 + .8*X_special[, 1] - .6*X_special[, 2])
  y_nb <- rnbinom(n, mu=mu_nb, size=2)
  # nb() estimates theta; nb(theta=2) would keep it fixed at two.
  nb_fit <- L0glm.fit(X_special, y_nb, family=mgcv::nb(),
    intercept=TRUE, lambda="gic", relaxed=TRUE, maxit=100, tol=1e-8)
  nb_fit$theta
  coef(nb_fit)
  nb_gic <- L0glm.tune(X_special, y_nb, family=mgcv::nb(),
    intercept=TRUE, tune="gic", nlambda=5,
    relaxed=TRUE, maxit=100, tol=1e-8)
  nb_cv <- L0glm.tune(X_special, y_nb, family=mgcv::nb(),
    intercept=TRUE, tune="cv", nlambda=5, nfolds=3, foldid=folds_special,
    relaxed=TRUE, maxit=100, tol=1e-8)
  c(GIC=nb_gic$lambda_best, CV=nb_cv$lambda_best, CV_1se=nb_cv$lambda_1se)
  predict(nb_cv$fit_1se, X_special[1:5, , drop=FALSE], type="response")

  # Three-category multinomial: only two predictor rows affect probabilities.
  eta <- cbind(0, .9*X_special[, 1] - .7*X_special[, 2],
    -.8*X_special[, 1] + .6*X_special[, 2])
  probabilities <- exp(eta - apply(eta, 1, max))
  probabilities <- probabilities/rowSums(probabilities)
  y_multinomial <- factor(vapply(seq_len(n), function(i)
    sample.int(3, 1, prob=probabilities[i, ]), integer(1)), levels=1:3)
  multinomial_fit <- L0glm.fit(X_special, y_multinomial,
    family=mgcv::multinom(K=2), lambda="gic", grouped=TRUE,
    relaxed=TRUE, maxit=200, tol=1e-8)
  coef(multinomial_fit)               # original-scale, zero-sum CLR rows
  multinomial_gic <- L0glm.tune(X_special, y_multinomial,
    family=mgcv::multinom(K=2), tune="gic", nlambda=5, grouped=TRUE,
    relaxed=TRUE, maxit=200, tol=1e-8)
  multinomial_cv <- L0glm.tune(X_special, y_multinomial,
    family=mgcv::multinom(K=2), tune="cv", nlambda=5,
    nfolds=3, foldid=folds_special, grouped=TRUE,
    relaxed=TRUE, maxit=200, tol=1e-8)
  c(GIC=multinomial_gic$lambda_best, CV=multinomial_cv$lambda_best,
    CV_1se=multinomial_cv$lambda_1se)
  predict(multinomial_cv$fit_1se, X_special[1:5, , drop=FALSE], type="response")

  # Right-censored Cox model: two nonzero log-hazard effects, no intercept.
  if (requireNamespace("survival", quietly=TRUE)) {
    event_time <- rexp(n, rate=exp(.7*X_special[, 1] - .5*X_special[, 2]))
    censor_time <- rexp(n, rate=.4)
    y_cox <- survival::Surv(pmin(event_time, censor_time),
      event_time <= censor_time)
    cox_fit <- L0glm.fit(X_special, y_cox, family=mgcv::cox.ph(),
      lambda="gic", relaxed=TRUE, maxit=100, tol=1e-8)
    coef(cox_fit)
    cox_gic <- L0glm.tune(X_special, y_cox, family=mgcv::cox.ph(),
      tune="gic", nlambda=5, relaxed=TRUE, maxit=100, tol=1e-8)
    cox_cv <- L0glm.tune(X_special, y_cox, family=mgcv::cox.ph(),
      tune="cv", nlambda=5, nfolds=3, foldid=folds_special,
      relaxed=TRUE, maxit=100, tol=1e-8)
    c(GIC=cox_gic$lambda_best, CV=cox_cv$lambda_best, CV_1se=cox_cv$lambda_1se)
    predict(cox_cv$fit_1se, X_special[1:5, , drop=FALSE], type="risk")
  }
  par(family_par)
}
}
